{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "@id": "https://kubaik.github.io/open-source-contributions-the-credibility-trap/#faq",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How many open source contributions do I need to get hired?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "There is no threshold, and anyone who gives you a number is guessing. What matters is whether the contributions demonstrate the skills the role requires. A single well-documented bug fix in a production-critical library can be more persuasive than dozens of documentation changes, because it shows you can reproduce a failure, reason about a fix, and communicate the trade-off. Focus on depth in one or two projects rather than breadth across many."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Should I contribute to a project I do not use at work?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes, if the project's problem domain matches the work you want to do. Contributing to a project you do not use is a way to learn a new domain, but it requires more upfront reading because you do not have operational context. The risk is that you misjudge what matters to users. Mitigate it by reading the issue tracker and the project's design documents before writing code, and by starting with a bug that has a clear reproduction."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What if my pull request is rejected?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A rejected pull request is not a failed contribution; it is a data point. Read the review comments carefully and ask whether the rejection was about the approach, the scope, or the timing. If the maintainer suggests a different approach, that is a signal about the project's priorities. If the rejection is unexplained, it may be a sign that the project is not a good fit for your goals. Either way, the interaction is public, and how you respond is part of your reputation."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do maintainers care about contributors who only submit small fixes?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Maintainers care about contributions that reduce their workload without creating new problems. A small fix that closes a real bug is valuable; a small fix that adds noise is not. The distinction is whether the change is grounded in a reproducible problem. If you want to be remembered, pair the fix with a test and a clear explanation of the failure mode. That combination is what makes a small contribution credible."
      }
    }
  ]
}
